  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "At a deep-sea sediment site, a sample is found with $30%$ of its original beryllium-10 content. Given that the half-life of beryllium-10 is $1.39$ million years, how old is the sample? Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\nThe sample is approximately\n{{field-1}}\nmillion years old.",
  "worked_solution": "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{\u2032}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=1.39$ million years, we have\n\n$$\n1.39=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}\\,\u21d2\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{1.39}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/1.39}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.30{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.30{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/1.39}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.30{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/1.39} \\\\ 0.30\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/1.39} \\\\ 0.30 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/1.39} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.30) & =ln\\,({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/1.39}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.30) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{1.39} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1.39\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.30)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\ t & \u22482.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $2.4$ million years old.",
